{
 "lead": "A real supply has inductance: transformer leakage, cables, the alternator's own reactance. When a thyristor is fired, the current cannot jump at once from the outgoing device to the incoming one, so both conduct together for an overlap angle μ. During the overlap the output loses a slice of voltage, so the DC voltage falls as the load current rises, the supply waveform is notched, and the converter's inverter range shrinks. Every line-commutated converter has this effect, and examiners ask for its derivation almost every year.",
 "prereq": "You need: the continuous-current waveforms of <a href=\"#single-phase-half-and-fully-controlled-rectifiers\">single-phase</a> and <a href=\"#three-phase-controlled-rectifiers\">three-phase controlled rectifiers</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Why the current overlaps",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Call the source inductance Ls, in henries. An inductor obeys v = Ls di/dt, so its current can change only at a finite rate. Take a single-phase bridge with a level load current Id (a large DC inductor), where Ls is the total inductance in the AC loop (leakage of the transformer referred to the secondary)."
    },
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "Before ωt = α, T3 and T4 carry Id and the supply current is −Id.",
      "At α, T1 and T2 are fired. The supply current must swing from −Id to +Id, but Ls holds it back.",
      "For a while all four thyristors conduct. The bridge output is short-circuited, so vo = 0, and the whole supply voltage appears across Ls: vs = Ls dis/dt.",
      "When is reaches +Id, T3 and T4 turn off at ωt = α + μ and vo follows vs again."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Integrating vs = Ls dis/dt over the overlap gives Vm[cos α − cos(α + μ)] = 2ωLs Id. In a three-phase bridge only two phases are shorted during each commutation, so the output sits halfway between the two phase voltages instead of at zero, but the idea is the same. Some syllabi call ωLs the commutating reactance."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Overlap angle and voltage drop",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "single-phase bridge:   cos α − cos(α + μ) = 2ωLs Id / Vm;   ΔVd = 2ωLs Id / π",
     "where": "ω = 2πf in rad/s; Ls is the total AC-side inductance in H; Id is the DC current in A; Vm is the supply peak in V; ΔVd is the average DC voltage lost, in V."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "three-phase bridge:   cos α − cos(α + μ) = 2ωLs Id / (√2 VL);   ΔVd = 3ωLs Id / π",
     "where": "Ls is the inductance per phase in H and VL the line RMS voltage in V. Six commutations per cycle instead of two give the factor 3 instead of 2."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Vdc = Vd0 cos α − ΔVd = (Vd0/2)[cos α + cos(α + μ)]",
     "where": "Vd0 is the no-load output at α = 0: 2Vm/π (single-phase) or 3√2 VL/π (three-phase)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "ΔVd/Id = 2ωLs/π or 3ωLs/π behaves like a resistance, the <b>equivalent commutating resistance</b> Rc. It drops voltage but dissipates nothing: the converter acts as an ideal source Vd0 cos α in series with Rc. The overlap grows with Id and Ls, and for a given current it is smallest near α = 90°, where the commutating voltage is largest. A DC smoothing inductor does not cause any of this; it only keeps Id level."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Notches, regulation and the inverter limit",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "During each overlap the supply terminals are shorted through the thyristors, so the voltage seen by other loads has notches, p of them per cycle. They disturb electronic loads, and a nearby converter that fires on a notch can misfire; large drives are therefore fed from their own transformer or through line reactors. The output falls with load (poor regulation), and the top of the range is lost: even at α = 0 the converter gives Vd0 − ΔVd."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "In inverter mode the overlap eats into the time the outgoing thyristor needs to recover, which sets the largest usable firing angle. That limit is worked out in <a href=\"#inversion-mode-and-the-inverter-angle-limit\">inversion mode and the inverter angle limit</a>."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "A three-phase fully controlled bridge runs from a 400 V, 50 Hz line with a source inductance of 1 mH per phase and a level load current of 20 A. Find ωLs, ΔVd, the output at α = 0 and the overlap angle at α = 0 and at α = 60°.",
   "steps": [
    "ω = 2π × 50 = 314.16 rad/s; ωLs = 314.16 × 0.001 = 0.3142 Ω.",
    "ΔVd = 3ωLs Id/π = 3 × 0.3142 × 20/π = 6.00 V.",
    "Vd0 = 3√2 × 400/π = 540.19 V, so at α = 0 Vdc = 540.19 − 6.00 = 534.19 V.",
    "2ωLs Id/(√2 VL) = 2 × 0.3142 × 20/565.69 = 0.0222. At α = 0: cos μ = 1 − 0.0222 = 0.9778, μ = 12.1°.",
    "At α = 60°: cos(60° + μ) = 0.5 − 0.0222 = 0.4778, so 60° + μ = 61.46° and μ = 1.46°. The same current overlaps far less near 90°."
   ],
   "answer": "ωLs = 0.314 Ω, ΔVd = 6.0 V, Vdc(α = 0) = 534.2 V, μ = 12.1° at α = 0 and 1.46° at α = 60°."
  },
  {
   "question": "A single-phase fully controlled bridge on 230 V, 50 Hz has a total source inductance Ls = 5 mH and carries a level 20 A at α = 30°. Find μ, ΔVd and Vdc.",
   "steps": [
    "Vm = 325.27 V; ωLs = 314.16 × 0.005 = 1.571 Ω.",
    "cos(α + μ) = cos 30° − 2ωLs Id/Vm = 0.8660 − (2 × 1.571 × 20)/325.27 = 0.8660 − 0.1932 = 0.6729, so α + μ = 47.71° and μ = 17.71°.",
    "ΔVd = 2ωLs Id/π = 2 × 1.571 × 20/π = 20.0 V.",
    "Vdc = 207.07 × 0.8660 − 20.0 = 179.33 − 20.0 = 159.33 V.",
    "Check with the second form: (Vm/π)[cos α + cos(α + μ)] = 103.54 × (0.8660 + 0.6729) = 159.33 V."
   ],
   "answer": "μ = 17.7°, ΔVd = 20.0 V, Vdc = 159.3 V instead of 179.3 V without Ls."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Blaming the DC smoothing choke for the overlap. Only inductance on the AC side delays the transfer of current.",
  "Forgetting the drop at α = 0. ΔVd is subtracted after the cosine term, so even full output loses it.",
  "Using 3ωLs Id/π for a single-phase bridge. It is 2ωLs Id/π there; 3 belongs to the six-pulse bridge.",
  "Putting the phase voltage or the peak into the three-phase overlap formula. It needs √2 VL, the line peak.",
  "Calling Rc a real resistor. It wastes no power; it only models the lost volt-seconds."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Define the overlap angle and state its cause.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "μ is the angle during which the outgoing and incoming devices conduct together. It is caused by the AC-side (source) inductance, which stops the line current from changing instantly."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why is the output of a single-phase bridge zero during overlap?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "All four thyristors conduct, so the bridge output terminals are shorted together through them; the supply voltage appears across the source inductance instead."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A single-phase bridge on 230 V, 50 Hz has Ls = 2 mH (total) and Id = 10 A. Find ΔVd and μ at α = 0.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "ωLs = 0.6283 Ω. ΔVd = 2 × 0.6283 × 10/π = 4.0 V. cos μ = 1 − 2 × 0.6283 × 10/325.27 = 0.9614, μ = 16.0°."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A six-pulse bridge has Vd0 = 540 V and Rc = 0.3 Ω. Find Vdc at α = 45° and Id = 100 A.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Vdc = 540 × 0.7071 − 0.3 × 100 = 381.8 − 30 = 351.8 V."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Where do the notches on the supply voltage of a thyristor drive come from, and who suffers?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "From the short circuit of two supply lines through the thyristors during each overlap, p times per cycle. Other loads on the same bus see a distorted voltage, and a nearby converter can misfire."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "Why is the overlap angle smaller at α = 60° than at α = 0 for the same current?",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "The current transfer needs a fixed volt-second area, 2ωLs Id. Near α = 90° the commutating voltage is at its peak, so the area is collected over a smaller angle: in the worked example μ falls from 12.1° at α = 0 to 1.46° at α = 60°."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. HVDC converter transformers are built with a deliberately chosen leakage reactance so that the commutating reactance limits fault currents without making the overlap too long. Grid-connected drives specify a line reactor in per cent impedance for the same reason. Search for 'commutation notch', 'line reactor per cent impedance' and 'converter transformer leakage reactance'.",
 "figure": {
